Robert Love (roblove@mailhost.world.net) wrote:
> Service provider Ausnet, whose invasion by Optic Surfer has
> had so much discussion in this group recently (yes, I've lurked my
> requisite month), appears to have deleted all of the obviously sexual
> newsgroups from their system.

Assuming the censorship didn't happen further up the news propagation
tree before it got to Ausnet, which happens sometimes.

It looks like yet another case of idiot management saying "get rid of
everything with sex in it" without realising that there are lots of
other newsgroups that contain information which makes a.b.p.e look like
a Sunday school picnic.  This type of censorship is the worst: it isn't
based on content, but rather on rumors of content.  It's like the
communities in the US (Florida I think) which banned NYPD Blue based on
the hype surrounding the "nude" scenes.  A survey done by a research
group showed that there were things already on TV in those communities
that made NYPD Blue look quite ordinary in comparison.

If Ausnet wants to protect the schools, they would be much better off
setting up a separate news server with just the "approved" newsgroups and
then using firewalls to prevent the kids accessing any news server except
the "safe" one.  Banning it from the adult server acheives nothing and
fools no one.  The most it will do is create more traffic on the link
to the rest of the world when users request it individually, denying other
users access to "normal" things.

As for liability, no IP provider in Australia has yet been sued for carrying
sex related newsgroups.  Until a court case occurs to set a precedent, banning
newsgroups is just giving into fear of currently non-existent threats.

I've played this game before with Melbourne University.  It will get you
exactly nowhere.  "It's our system so bugger off" sums up their response
fairly nicely.  Sadly, too many people think that the only adults who would
be interested in seeing such newsgroups propagated are perverts.  It is
difficult to convince them of the errors in such reasoning.

Cheers,

