Carl Kadie has listed some perceived dangers with the 
labeling of documents proposals. Most of the dangers can be
avoided, provided:

1. the labeling scheme is truly voluntary 
(no one said "all" materials "have" to be labelled.
Carl doesn't have to label his pages. His pages have no label 
and are hence labeled "unlabeled" by browsers equipped to use 
labels :-) (Hope he doesn't mind!)  

2. the labeling is done by authors, not providers or carriers or others
(sure, its subjective and incomplete and has no guarantees, but 
authors can choose to meet some social obligations by flagging 
materials as potentially offensive if they so wish.)

3. The voluntary labeling in the record industry is a case where
voluntary labeling didn't work. What happened was that record
stores succumbed to intense pressure not to carry records with
certain labels. Would this happen to internet providers? 

3.1 Already does to a certain extent...newsgroup names are
self-labeling and some providers succumb to pressure to
remove them. It is possible voluntary labeling may exacerbate
this... 

3.2 If censorship by carriers based on labels became widespread, 
this would provide an incentive for authors to voluntarily
mislabel, or just not label their materials, and the voluntary 
labeling system would breakdown. The advantages of flagging
content is lost, but at least the censors don't win...

A prerequisite for voluntary labeling to work is that there is no 
or only isolated censorship by internet providers without consent
of their readers. 
Perhaps there is a nice symbiosis between "no censorship by
carriers/providers" and voluntary labeling....One needs the other
to survive.

regards Rohan
